    "detail": "Anonymous Appalachian song in oral circulation in the southern mountains through the nineteenth century; the tune descends from the British ballad air known as 'Matty Groves', printed in nineteenth-century collections.",
    "sourceEdition": "Traditional two-strain old-time setting of the tune in its modal A form with the flat-seventh change, reduced to melody and changes",
    "notes": "Melody and harmony only, no lyrics. Encoded as the traditional modal old-time version, not any modern recorded arrangement. Mode is written as minor with a natural (flat) seventh degree throughout, which is the tune's modal character and not a missing leading tone; the raised sixth of the dorian reading is absent from the melody, as it is in most sung versions."
